  "description": "Add-on for IntentSharp: runs typed, checked LLM calls on any Microsoft.Extensions.AI IChatClient, such as Ollama or Azure OpenAI, and gives catalog intents to Agent Framework agents as AIFunction tools. An entry of the chat-client kind in the provider file names a chat client registered in dependency injection. UseChatClient makes a chat client the primary provider and UseChatClientAsFallback the fallback, beside or instead of the kinds the provider file names. Validation does not depend on the backend: structured output that does not match your type or your rules fails its attempt, whatever the backend did, and the call retries with the findings, within your limits. AsAIFunctions turns each enabled catalog intent into an AIFunction tool, for a Microsoft Agent Framework agent or ChatOptions.Tools, that answers with a value only when the result passed the intent's checks, and with an error that lists the problems otherwise, so each answer is checked before the agent sees it. A failed request to the chat client itself is not retried by IntentSharp: put those retries in the chat client's own pipeline, or rely on the fallback. Add this package when your model is reached through an IChatClient or an agent should call intents as tools; it brings IntentSharp and IntentSharp.Catalog with it. A project whose provider file names only the openai and anthropic kinds, and that gives no intents to agents, does not need it, and the IntentSharp package itself does not depend on Microsoft.Extensions.AI. Our estimate for IntentSharp in a typical business application is 20 to 40% less application code. Commercial software, not open source: evaluate free for 30 days on development and test systems; after that, business use needs a paid license, and production use needs one from the first day (intentsharp.com/pricing).",
